## **2****.4****. ****Comparative analysis of proposed study with existing studies**

Within the buildings sector, a comparative analysis was conducted on 20 studies as part of this research proposal, analyzing the objectives of recent publications with the objectives of this proposed study, to show the severe lack in studies considering hydrogen in the buildings sector. The findings are as follows:

9 studies (Bashir et al., 2019; Cedillos Alvarado et al., 2016; Huang et al., 2019; Huang et al., 2020; Lima and Feijão, 2022; Lu et al., 2021; Mariaud et al., 2017; Mehleri et al., 2013; Yan et al., 2020) employed MILP research methods for single-objective studies where hydrogen was not considered as part of the analysis in the buildings sector. 

2 studies (Huang et al., 2021; Shuai et al., 2019) used NLP as the research method for their studies, but they did not focus on hydrogen as part of their analysis in the sector.

1 study (Berger et al., 2020) utilized LP for a single-objective study on hydrogen integration in the buildings sector, this was the only study that investigated the molecule in its research focus. 

1 study (Qin et al., 2020) employed LP but did not consider the fuel as part of its research scope.

2 studies (Huang et al., 2021; Montoya et al., 2020) conducted MINLP for single-objective studies that did not consider hydrogen in the sector. 

5 studies (Arsalis, 2019; Bartolini et al., 2020; Deymi-Dashtebayaz et al., 2019; Gondal & Sahir, 2012; Ozçelep et al., 2021) employed different methodologies, for each of their research scopes, and focused on the potential role of hydrogen, they considered the role of hydrogen from 1-20% of the sector’s demand.

In contrast, the study at the heart of this proposal aims to explore the topic of hydrogen and its potential penetration in the buildings sector using the LP/MILP method across the entire demand range (0% to 100% range) in the EU and Switzerland.

# **Chapter ****3****: Conclusion**** and timeline**

With the aim of bridging the gap and supporting scientific knowledge on the potential role of hydrogen in the buildings sector for the a 2050 NZE in the EU and Switzerland, this proposal has identified a visible research gap and provides three main research questions that it aims to answer through the methodology proposed in the detailed research plan. The methodology is characterised by two key elements. Firstly, with the intent of not using black box environments, a sufficient portion of the methodology is dedicated to writing, through the LEAP environment, a model and linear optimisation that are fully transparent to any interested stakeholder. Secondly, providing ample time for feedback loop iterations is expected to propel the complexity and usefulness of the model and optimisation framework. The insights generated through this research will shed important insights into the most effective solutions for hydrogen adoption in the sector for the selected regions. The results of the research will to contribute to bridging the research gap and increasing awareness amongst decision-makers and energy experts. 

