| **Symbol** | **Significance** | **Symbol** | **Significance** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| fC | Total investment cost | H | Hydrogen |
| Cinst | Installation cost of energy supply equipment | HP | Heat Pump |
| Com | Operation & maintenance cost | ∆ | Stands for the time interval of (t) |
| Cfuel | Cost for the consumed fuels | t | Time |
| Cedpur | Cost for purchasing energy demand energy from the utility grid | d | Day |
| Cedsal | Revenue from selling energy demand energy to the grid | ∝d,t,tech,k | Defined as the operating level k of the technology tech at (d) and (t) |
| Csubs | Power generation subsidies for distributed energy systems | Otech,ked | Explained as the end demand (ed) output of (tech) at operating level (k) |
| CCO2 | Income from selling carbon emission allowances | ηtech,ktech,es | Represents the efficiency of the (tech) for (ed) at (k) |
| CRF | Capital Recovery Factor | p | The price |
| r | Signifies the interest rate | Efuel | Emissions from energy produced and consumed within the same country and destined to the buildings sector |
| m | Stands for the lifespan of each component | Eedpur | Associated emissions beyond the country’s borders (EU single market, or energy imports beyond that) |
| τtech | Choice limit of particular technologies | ∅ | Optimization constraint |
| ctechtechn,inst | Installation cost of the given technology | fnmin | Corresponds to the optimum values of fn when independently optimized |
| ctechtechn, om | Pertains to the maintenance of the specific technology | ωn | The analogous weight coefficients, which reflect the preferences of the decision-makers. |
| FF | Fossil Fuels | β | Technology optimization constraint |

Source: Based on author’s definition

## **Appendix 5**** – ****Possible calibration tools**

**Table ****5****: ****P****ossible ****Analytical tools and techniques**** for the calibration process**

| **Calibration Techniques** | **Description** |
| --- | --- |
| Sensitivity analysis | Sensitivity analysis is employed to gain insights into how variations in uncertain inputs correspond to outputs. This method can help pinpoint insignificant parameters that are overlooked during calibration, or aid in prioritizing future efforts, such as identifying crucial parameters for measurements or in-depth investigations. |
| High-resolution data | High-resolution data employs information at hourly or sub-hourly intervals, as opposed to daily or monthly time frames. |
| Audits | Research detailed audits conducted on current case-studies in the building systems or sub-systems. |
| Uncertainty quantification | Assessing parameter's uncertainty through error analysis such as through the use of predicative density. |
| Expert feedback | Incorporating expert judgment and knowledge into the optimisation or model assumptions. |
| Parameter reduction | Parameter reduction entails decreasing the number of model parameters. Examples include day-typing, where detailed schedules are condensed into typical day-type schedules; and zone-typing, which groups similar regions and zones. |
| Base-load modelling | Base-load modelling utilizes measured base-loads for calibrating building models. |

Source: Adapted from literature (Chong et al., 2021; Vidoni, 2001)

